---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, SUBJECT, GITHUB_REPO, REPORT_FORM_URL } from '../config';

const subjectPath = `/${SUBJECT.path}/`;
const repoUrl = `https://github.com/${GITHUB_REPO}`;
const reportsGoToForm = REPORT_FORM_URL !== '';
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`About ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description={`What ${SITE_NAME} is: free, original exam-style topical questions for ${SUBJECT.fullName}, with mark schemes and worked solutions, how they were made, and how to report an error.`}
  canonicalPath="/about/"
>
  <div class="shell">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>About {SITE_NAME}</h1>
      <p>
        {SITE_NAME} is a free collection of exam-style practice questions for
        <a href={subjectPath}>{SUBJECT.fullName}</a> (the syllabus for exams in {SUBJECT.syllabusYears}),
        sorted by topic. Every question comes with a mark scheme and a worked solution, hidden until you
        choose to look, so you can attempt the question first and then mark your own answer.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the questions come from</h2>
      <p>
        The questions are original. They are written in the style of the exam so that practising them
        feels like the real thing, but they are not past papers, and nothing is copied from Cambridge
        question papers or mark schemes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every question, mark scheme and worked solution was written and checked with AI tools. No teacher
        or examiner has reviewed them. Mistakes are possible: if anything looks wrong, use the Report an
        error link under that question.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting an error</h2>
      <p>
        Each question has a Report an error link. It opens a short report with the question's id and web
        address already filled in, so you only need to say what looks wrong.
        {reportsGoToForm
          ? ' The report goes to a form, and nothing on the form identifies you unless you choose to add your details.'
          : ' Until the report form is ready, the link opens a new issue on the public GitHub repository, which needs a GitHub account.'}
      </p>
      <p>
        Reports are read, and corrections are published to the site. The site is built in a public
        repository <a href={repoUrl} rel="noopener">on GitHub</a>, so every change to a question is visible.
      </p>

      <h2>Independence</h2>
      <p>
        {SITE_NAME} is independent and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Cambridge University Press
        &amp; Assessment. The qualification is named only to say what the questions are for.
      </p>

      <h2>Free, with no accounts</h2>
      <p>
        Everything on the site is free to use. There is nothing to sign up for and nothing to download.
        The site keeps no record of what you answer; your score on a page lasts only as long as the page is
        open. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for the full list, which is short.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
